  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/4adc01d460f42f134f2e6c8da1e2fe516c8e6cfb64bcfeb7a5db89cf93f1fc11.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-4.$ Find the vector $Cv.$",
  "worked_solution": "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png)",
